\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Derivative signs and degrees at every grid size}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Take #{n=1}, #{N\ge 5} and #{f(p)=\sin p-c\sin 2p} with
#{h=2\pi /N} and #{\tfrac{1}{2}<c<1/(2\cos h)}. Since #{0<h<\tfrac{\pi}{2}},
this interval is nonempty. The derivative at zero is #{f'(0)=1-2c<0}.
But #{f(\pm h)=\pm\sin h(1-2c\cos h)}, so the sampled interpolant crosses
from negative to positive and has vertex degree #{+1} by \ref{fdip-013Z}.
The equation #{\cos p_*=1/(2c)} has a solution #{0<p_*<h} because
#{\cos h<1/(2c)<1}. Thus the underlying symbol has a further zero
on each side of the origin, strictly before the neighboring sample. Such a #{c} exists for every period #{N\ge 5}, which extends the case #{N=8},
#{c=3/5} of \ref{fdip-0142} to all these periods. Thus for every period #{N\ge 5} the sign of the derivative of a range-#{2} symbol at a zero vertex need not equal the degree of that vertex for the sampled interpolant: here the derivative is negative and the degree is #{+1}.}

\p{Fix now #{c>\tfrac{1}{2}} independently of #{N}, still with
#{N\ge5}, and put #{p_*=\arccos(1/(2c))}, so
#{0<p_*<\tfrac{\pi}{2}}. The same formula for #{f(\pm h)}
gives vertex degree #{+1} when #{h>p_*} and #{-1} when
#{h<p_*}. Thus no fixed #{c} gives the mismatch at every
period. At #{h=p_*}, the samples at #{-h,0,h} all vanish:
the interpolant is zero on both adjacent edges, and the origin has
no nondegenerate [vertex degree](fdip-013Y). For #{c=3/5},
#{\cos(\pi/5)<5/6<\cos(\pi/6)} places #{N=10} on the
first branch and #{N=12} on the second.}

\p{When #{h<p_*}, the two sampled crossings beside the origin
each contribute #{+1}. Indeed,
#{f(p)=\sin p(1-2c\cos p)} is negative before #{p_*}
and positive after it in #{(0,\pi)}. Consecutive samples
bracketing #{p_*} therefore give an upward affine crossing;
if #{p_*} is a sample, its two neighbors instead give
vertex degree #{+1} by \ref{fdip-013Z}. Oddness gives the
other upward crossing. The edge crossings need not occur at
the continuous zeros #{\pm p_*}.}

\p{If #{4} divides #{N}, both endpoints of
#{[-\pi/2,\pi/2]} are samples, with values #{-1} and #{1}.
Choose a [regular value](fdip-013Y) #{y} with #{-1<y<1}.
On the oriented edge from #{p} to #{p+h}, the signed crossing is
##{\frac{\operatorname{sgn}(f(p+h)-y)
-\operatorname{sgn}(f(p)-y)}2.}
Summing over that interval cancels every interior sample and
gives #{(1-(-1))/2=1}. This also holds at #{h=p_*}, when
the zero vertices are degenerate. The endpoints remain samples
on every integer-multiple refinement, so this interval count
stays #{+1} for the fixed symbol.}
